| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run1) | 936.62 | 0.27 | 1 specimen only, SD=0 (single fit), minor wrinkles |
| 17p5-19deg-90s-N2-1800s (run1) | 860.94 | 0.28 | 1 specimen, SD=0 (single fit), wrinkle |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run2) | 675.14 | 0.30 | 2 runs, top-edge gap + wrinkles |
| 17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s (run6) | 658.85 | 0.31 | Clean membrane, good quality |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run3) | 421.45 | 0.44 | Minor wrinkles |
| 18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s (run5) | 468.22 | 0.40 | Wrinkles |
| 17-10deg-60s-N2-1800s (run2) | 402.35 | 0.31 | Clean membrane |
| 18p5-12deg-120s-N2-1800s (run1) | 610.89 | 0.37 | High CV=0.36, wrinkles |
| 17p5-17deg-75s-NoN2-1800s (run3) | 428.13 | 0.35 | Wrinkle |
| 17p5-17deg-75s-NoN2-1800s (run1) | 486.96 | 0.33 | Clean membrane |

**Approximate Pareto front** (non-dominated in modulus × pore fraction):
- **936 bar / 0.27** — run1 only, SD=0 (single fit), not independently reproduced
- **860 bar / 0.28** — run1 only, SD=0 (single fit), not independently reproduced  
- **658 bar / 0.31** — run6, clean membrane, but run1 (936) and run2 (675) show high variability across runs
- **610 bar / 0.37** — run1 of 18p5-12deg, high CV=0.36, wrinkles, not reproduced
- **421–468 bar / 0.40–0.44** — 18-15deg-600s runs, moderate modulus, high pore fraction

### Validation status

- **17p5-18deg-75s-N2-1800s**: runs 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 exist. Run1 (936 bar, 0.27) is a single-fit outlier (SD=0). Run2 (675, 0.30), run6 (659, 0.31) are more consistent. Runs 4 and 5 have no modulus data. The condition has been run 6 times but modulus varies enormously (675–936 across measured runs), and run1 is a single-specimen outlier. The mean of measured runs (run1, run2, run6) is ~757 bar / 0.29 — but run1 is suspect.

- **18p5-12deg-120s-N2-1800s**: runs 1 and 2 exist. Run1: 611 bar / 0.37 (CV=0.36, high scatter). Run2: 382 bar / 0.38 (single fit). Very inconsistent modulus across runs; pore fraction is consistent (~0.37–0.38). This is a genuinely interesting high-pore-fraction candidate but modulus is unreliable.

- **18-15deg-600s-NoN2-1350s**: 5 runs total. Modulus ranges 340–476 bar, pore fraction 0.32–0.44. High variability. Not a strong modulus candidate but consistently high pore fraction.

### Key observations and gaps

The recent campaign has heavily explored **polymer_wt=17–17.5, bath_temp=10–20°C, mixing_temp=65°C, pullcast_speed=10, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=60–120s, N2=true**. This region is well-sampled.

**Under-sampled promising regions:**
1. **Higher polymer_wt (18.5–19) with moderate bath_temp (14–16°C)** — only 18p5-12deg and 18-14deg have been tried recently. The 18p5-12deg condition shows high pore fraction (~0.37–0.38) but inconsistent modulus. Moving to bath_temp=15°C with polymer_wt=18.5 is unexplored.
2. **Lower pullcast_speed with higher polymer_wt** — most recent experiments use speed=10. Lower speed (e.g., 6–8) with polymer_wt=18–18.5 and bath_temp=14–16°C is largely unexplored in the current campaign.
3. **The 18p5-12deg condition** needs a clean repeat to clarify whether the high pore fraction (~0.37–0.38) is real and whether modulus can be reliably measured.

### Decision

The **18p5-12deg-120s-N2-1800s** condition (polymer_wt=18.5, bath_temp=12, pullcast_speed=8, mixing_temp=65, coupon_to_bath_wait_time=120, N2=true, nips_bath_wait_time=1800) showed:
- Run1: 611 bar / 0.37 (CV=0.36, wrinkles, high scatter)
- Run2: 382 bar / 0.38 (single fit, wrinkles)

The pore fraction is consistently ~0.37–0.38 (among the highest with measured modulus), but modulus is highly variable and both runs had wrinkles. This is a Pareto-interesting region (high pore fraction, potentially moderate-to-high modulus) that needs clarification.

However, rather than repeating a wrinkle-prone condition, I'll make a **modest exploration** nearby: **polymer_wt=18.5, bath_temp=14°C** (slightly warmer bath, which has helped reduce wrinkling in other conditions), **pullcast_speed=8** (same as 18p5-12deg), **coupon_to_bath_wait_time=90s** (slightly shorter than 120s, which has been associated with cleaner membranes in the 17–18 wt% range), **N2=true**, **mixing_temp=65**, **nips_bath_wait_time=1800**. This is a genuinely new point (18.5 wt%, bath_temp=14°C has not been tried) that could clarify whether the high pore fraction of the 18.5 wt% system persists at a slightly warmer bath temperature while potentially improving membrane quality and modulus.

**Expected benefit:** Higher polymer concentration (18.5 wt%) tends to produce higher pore fractions in this system (as seen in 18p5-12deg runs). A slightly warmer bath (14°C vs 12°C) slows phase separation slightly, which may improve membrane flatness and modulus while preserving elevated pore fraction. This explores a new (polymer_wt, bath_temp) combination that could advance the Pareto front.

